# Server
#
# 127.0.0.1, not 0.0.0.0.  A Quick Tunnel dials the app in over loopback from
# the same machine, so a wildcard bind buys nothing and exposes the app -- and
# therefore /websockify and the signed-in Chrome behind it -- on every interface
# the Codespace has.  Change this only for a container network you control.
HOST=127.0.0.1
PORT=8000
DATA_DIR=./data

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Authentication (Google sign-in)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The app signs users in with Google through the Cloudflare Worker at the stable
# workers.dev origin.  The Worker runs the OIDC flow, stores users and sessions
# in D1 and sets the rag_session cookie; this backend is a resource server that
# trusts the Worker only when the request carries RAG_INTERNAL_TOKEN.
#
# Locally (`npm run dev` in Frontend/) the Vite dev server proxies /auth to this
# process, so the same variables drive a direct OIDC login with no Worker.
#
# Create an OAuth client of type "Web application" in the Google Cloud Console
# and register the redirect URIs you actually use:
#   https://rag-agents.<account>.workers.dev/auth/callback   (production)
#   http://localhost:8000/auth/callback                      (local)
# Sign-in asks only for the openid, email and profile scopes, so it works
# independently of any Gmail authorization -- Gmail is a separate, later consent
# reached from Settings, not part of login.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# The stable public origin, used to build the OAuth redirect URI.  Empty means
# "derive from the incoming request" (fine locally; set it in production).
PUBLIC_BASE_URL=

# Shared secret between the Cloudflare Worker and this backend.  When set, every
# protected route requires the Worker to have sent it, which is what stops a
# leaked Quick Tunnel URL from reaching the API directly.  Must equal the
# Worker's `wrangler secret put RAG_INTERNAL_TOKEN`.  Leave empty for local
# development with no Worker in front.
RAG_INTERNAL_TOKEN=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Gmail connection (a separate grant from sign-in)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Signing in asks only for openid/email/profile.  Connecting a mailbox is a
# second, explicit consent that adds exactly two read/send scopes:
#   https://www.googleapis.com/auth/gmail.send
#   https://www.googleapis.com/auth/gmail.readonly
# No Drive scope, and no Gmail modify/compose/delete scope, is ever requested.
# A mailbox without that second grant still signs in and works -- the mailbox is
# simply "not connected".
#
# The refresh token is stored encrypted at rest with AES-GCM under
# APP_ENCRYPTION_KEY (a fresh nonce per value); it is never sent to the browser,
# never placed in a model prompt and never written to a log.  Consent is bound
# to the same Google subject as the session, so authorizing a different Google
# account is rejected rather than stored.
#
# If the grant is revoked, or the token can no longer be read, the connection is
# marked and the user is told to reconnect; a revoked grant is never retried.
# Overrides for the Google endpoints (defaults are Google's production hosts):
GOOGLE_REVOKE_URL=https://oauth2.googleapis.com/revoke
# How long before an access token's expiry it is refreshed proactively.
GMAIL_REFRESH_SKEW_SECONDS=60

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Owner notifications (a dedicated, bot-owned sender)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# An agent may email ITS OWNER -- and nobody else -- for example to report a
# scheduled task's result.  This is deliberately NOT the owner's connected Gmail
# from the section above: the owner's mailbox is a personal credential, and using
# it would mean an assistant could send mail as the user.  Instead the server
# holds one dedicated bot mailbox that every notification is sent from.
#
# How the recipient and sender are decided:
#   * the recipient is the signed-in owner's verified email, resolved from the
#     session's user row -- never from a tool argument, a message body or a web
#     page, so no prompt injection can redirect a message to someone else;
#   * the sender (From) is the mailbox configured here, and the Gmail API call is
#     addressed to that mailbox's own account, so nothing can override it;
#   * the model can set only the subject and the body of a draft.  It cannot name
#     a recipient or add Cc/Bcc/From/Reply-To; such a call is refused outright;
#   * if no bot sender is configured, notifications fail closed (a clear refusal)
#     rather than falling back to the owner's Gmail or any other path.
#
# How to configure it -- choose ONE provider:
#
#   Gmail API (preferred when the bot mailbox is a Google account):
#     1. Create/find the bot Google account (e.g. bot@yourdomain or a dedicated
#        Gmail).  Do NOT use the owner's personal account.
#     2. Do a one-time OAuth consent for that account with the gmail.send scope,
#        using the same OAuth client as GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID/SECRET above, then take
#        the resulting refresh token.
#     3. Set BOT_SENDER_PROVIDER=gmail, BOT_SENDER_EMAIL, and
#        BOT_SENDER_REFRESH_TOKEN (see the encryption note below).
#
#   SMTP (any mailbox / provider):
#     Set BOT_SENDER_PROVIDER=smtp, BOT_SENDER_EMAIL, BOT_SMTP_HOST,
#     BOT_SMTP_PORT, BOT_SMTP_USER and BOT_SMTP_PASSWORD.  For a Gmail/Workspace
#     account use an app password, not the account password.
#
# The refresh token and the SMTP password are secrets.  Store them in the server
# environment only -- never in the frontend, never a VITE_* variable, never a
# commit.  They may be given either as a plain environment value or, preferably,
# as an AES-GCM value encrypted under APP_ENCRYPTION_KEY (the "v1." form that
# encrypt_secret produces), so a leaked environment file is not by itself a
# usable credential.
#
# If provider is left empty, it is inferred: a refresh token means gmail, an
# SMTP host means smtp.  Anything incomplete fails closed.
BOT_SENDER_PROVIDER=
BOT_SENDER_EMAIL=
BOT_SENDER_REFRESH_TOKEN=
BOT_SMTP_HOST=
BOT_SMTP_PORT=587
BOT_SMTP_USER=
BOT_SMTP_PASSWORD=
BOT_SMTP_USE_TLS=1
BOT_SMTP_USE_SSL=0

# HMAC key that hashes session ids, and the key that encrypts stored Gmail
# refresh tokens.  Generate each with:
#   python -c "import secrets;print(secrets.token_urlsafe(48))"
SESSION_SECRET=
APP_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# Provider keys. Empty key = provider disabled (falls back to mock).
OPENAI_API_KEY=
OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://api.openai.com/v1
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=https://api.anthropic.com
GOOGLE_API_KEY=
GEMINI_BASE_URL=https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com
XAI_API_KEY=
XAI_BASE_URL=https://api.x.ai/v1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Computer control (the AI driving the real remote browser)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Put the key HERE, on the server, in backend/.env -- which is what
# config.py::_load_dotenv reads.  Never in the frontend, never in a VITE_*
# variable, and never committed: everything under VITE_ is shipped to the
# browser in plain text, so a key placed there is a key published to whoever
# loads the page.
#
# In a GitHub Codespace the better place is a Codespaces secret, named exactly
# `OPENROUTER_API_KEY`, added under Codespaces > Settings > Secrets.  A real
# environment variable always wins over the file, so a Codespaces secret also
# overrides anything stale left in backend/.env.  Restart the backend after
# either change.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=

# The model must accept images: it is shown a screenshot of the screen on every
# turn after the first.  A text-only model will answer confidently and never
# once mention that it cannot see, so this is worth checking against the
# provider's model list before blaming the loop.
OPENROUTER_MODEL=
OPENROUTER_BASE_URL=https://openrouter.ai/api/v1

# --- Mistral, the second computer-control provider -----------------------
# A peer of OpenRouter, not a separate control loop: the same prompt, the same
# conversation, the same screenshot, the same parser and the same executors.
# Which one answers is chosen in the Computer Chat panel, per run and between
# turns, and it is recorded on every request in the trace.
#
# Same rules as the key above: server-side only, never in the frontend, never a
# VITE_* variable. In a Codespace, add it as a Codespaces secret named exactly
# `MISTRAL_API_KEY`.
#
# Every provider here is configured independently, so any one can be set without
# the others. Whichever is missing shows as "not configured" in the selector and is
# refused by name, rather than failing later as an unattributed error.
MISTRAL_API_KEY=
MISTRAL_BASE_URL=https://api.mistral.ai/v1

# Must accept images, for the same reason OPENROUTER_MODEL must. Defaults to
# mistral-small-2506, which does, so leaving this blank works -- a key with no
# model named is a missing variable rather than a request for a text-only model.
MISTRAL_MODEL=mistral-small-2506

# --- Groq, the third computer-control provider ----------------------------
# Same rules as Mistral above, for the same reasons: server-side only, never in
# the frontend, never a VITE_* variable, and independent of the other two so any
# one can be set without the others.  In a Codespace, add it as a Codespaces
# secret named exactly `GROQ_API_KEY`.
GROQ_API_KEY=
GROQ_BASE_URL=https://api.groq.com/openai/v1

# Must accept images, like every other provider here: the loop sends the newest
# screenshot on every turn after the first.  Defaults to qwen/qwen3.8-27b, which
# does, so a blank here works -- a key with no model named is a missing variable
# rather than a request for a text-only model.
GROQ_MODEL=qwen/qwen3.8-27b

# Router role for the above. Normally left alone: it exists so computer control
# can be pointed at a different provider without touching the others. Must be
# `openrouter`, `mistral` or `groq`; anything else is refused.
COMPUTER_PROVIDER=openrouter

# Limits on the loop, in steps. Small on purpose: this is a machine that can
# click, and a loop that can run a hundred clicks can empty a card.
COMPUTER_MAX_STEPS=12
COMPUTER_MAX_JSON_RETRIES=2

# How long to wait after an action before taking the next screenshot, in
# milliseconds. Without it the model is shown a half-painted page and clicks a
# target that is not there yet, which is the most common cause of a control loop
# that looks like it is working and is not.
COMPUTER_SETTLE_MS=1400
COMPUTER_SETTLE_MS_CLICK=900

# Budget, in milliseconds, that a state-changing action may take to stop
# changing the page.  Unlike the delays above, which wait a fixed time, this is
# measured: the page is polled until two consecutive reads agree or the budget
# runs out, so the state read that follows is of a page that has finished
# reacting.
COMPUTER_STATE_SETTLE_MS=600

# Where "search" goes. DuckDuckGo by default; the query is URL-encoded onto the
# end of this.  Read by the *agent* process on the remote machine rather than by
# the backend, so it has to be in the agent's environment (codespace/.env or the
# shell it starts in) to take effect.
COMPUTER_SEARCH_URL=https://duckduckgo.com/?q=

# Router. Provider names: openai | anthropic | gemini | grok | mock
COMMANDER_PROVIDER=anthropic
COMMANDER_MODEL=claude-sonnet-4
WORKER_PROVIDER=openai
WORKER_MODEL=gpt-4o
BROWSER_PROVIDER=gemini
BROWSER_MODEL=gemini-2.0-flash

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The remote computer (GitHub Codespace)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# There is no VM any more. The agent in backend/vm_agent/daemon.py runs on the
# Codespace itself and serves the three apps -- browser, files, terminal.
# Set this only when the backend and the agent are on different machines; when
# they share one Codespace the default is already right.
WORKSPACE_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:9000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The live screen -- Google sign-in, and the tunnel sits behind the Worker
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A Cloudflare Quick Tunnel puts this server on a public trycloudflare.com URL,
# and the Computer view is a live, signed-in, keyboard-driven Chrome.  The public
# front door is the Cloudflare Worker (worker/), which requires a Google session
# before it proxies anything here; the tunnel is its upstream, not the entry
# point.
#
# The tunnel URL is still worth keeping private: with RAG_INTERNAL_TOKEN set, a
# direct caller without the token and without a session is refused -- but there
# is no reason to hand out the address.  codespace/boot.sh prints it; do not post
# it.  Restart the tunnel (a Quick Tunnel picks a new random origin) to move it.
#
# The loopback ports are still not published -- 5900, 6080 and 9000 bind to
# 127.0.0.1 only, and the app is the only route to them.

# Where the tunnel script records the hostname cloudflared handed it.  The
# Quick Tunnel picks a random origin on every restart, so the app reads it from
# here and hands it to the browser via /screen/config rather than hardcoding it.
# Keep it in sync with PUBLIC_URL_FILE in codespace/env.sh.
PUBLIC_URL_FILE=/tmp/ragdesktop/public-url

# x11vnc's RFB port and websockify's WebSocket port, both loopback-only.  The
# tunnel publishes neither: it publishes this app, and the app proxies
# /websockify to SCREEN_WS_PORT after checking the session cookie.
SCREEN_VNC_ENABLED=1
SCREEN_VNC_PORT=5900
SCREEN_WS_PORT=6080

# The Vite build to serve from this process, so the app and the screen are one
# origin.  Same host for the page and the WebSocket is what lets the session
# cookie ride along with the upgrade.  Leave empty to auto-detect
# ../Frontend/dist, and to fall back to the old bundled prototype if missing.
# FRONTEND_DIST=../Frontend/dist

# Tool limits
MAX_TEXT_CHARS=8000
MAX_WEB_CHARS=12000
MAX_ITERS=4

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Restricted browsing for ordinary agents
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Ordinary agents (commander, workers, email, summary) must not share the
# computer-control browser or the personal Chrome profile, and their web access
# must pass a real allowlist enforced on the wire, not a URL string check.
#
# With this on, the ordinary-agent registry offers `browse` instead of the
# unrestricted `browser`, and `browse`, `fetch_url`, `web_search` and `shell` are
# routed through the restricted runtime.  Each refuses rather than falling back
# to the personal browser or the host, so an unreachable service is a refusal,
# never a quiet widening of access.  Off is the default so an un-configured
# checkout still runs; the Codespace launcher turns it on.
#
# Turn it on with an EMPTY allowlist and ordinary agents can browse nowhere,
# which is the intended default-deny.  Approve destinations with the owner-only
# tool:
#
#   sudo codespace/restricted-policy.sh add example.com
#   sudo codespace/restricted-policy.sh test https://example.com/page
RESTRICTED_BROWSING=0

# The allowlist.  /etc/rag-restricted/allowlist is root-owned and mode 0644, so
# the unprivileged agent can read it and cannot rewrite it.  Empty falls back to
# backend/restricted_allowlist.txt (the draft copy in this checkout).
RESTRICTED_ALLOWLIST_PATH=

# The allowlisting forward proxy.  In the deployment it runs in the sandbox and
# is the browser's and the shell's only egress; the firewall drops every other
# outbound packet, so a browser that ignores its own check still cannot leave.
RESTRICTED_GATEWAY_URL=http://127.0.0.1:9111

# The separate browser service: its own profile directory and its own port,
# neither of which is the computer-control browser's.  Never point this at the
# personal Chrome.
RESTRICTED_BROWSER_URL=http://127.0.0.1:9110
RESTRICTED_PROFILE_DIR=/tmp/rag-restricted-profile

# The shell for ordinary agents.  It must run inside the same network sandbox as
# the browser, or one `curl` walks around the gateway.  Empty means "not
# deployed": the shell tool then refuses while restricted browsing is on, which
# is the honest answer -- it does not silently run on the unrestricted host.
RESTRICTED_SHELL_URL=

# The Cloudflare Worker origin (the one signed in to).  When set, routine tools
# talk to its internal routines endpoints. If empty, it falls back to
# PUBLIC_BASE_URL.
WORKER_ORIGIN=

# Deploy the sandbox (Docker) that makes the above a boundary rather than a
# suggestion, then verify it:
#   codespace/restricted-sandbox.sh up && codespace/restricted-sandbox.sh verify

